    },
    {
      "text": "what was most interesting is how much how well the agent understood experiments we've tried before that didn't work.",
      "timestamp_sec": 795,
      "why": "Identifies negative-result retention as the underrated benefit of log-centric agents."
    },
    {
      "text": "I've done a lot of YOLO agents where you just like keep trying things and then it works, you're like, \"Yeah.\" But then I don't know the stuff that we tried that didn't work.",
      "timestamp_sec": 859,
      "why": "Names the failure mode that policy-gated experimentation fixes."
    },
    {
      "text": "my hypothesis is that that's in the training data. And there's just much less training data around how to build LLM based agents.",
      "timestamp_sec": 894,
      "why": "A testable explanation for why models code better in an old architectural idiom."
    },
    {
      "text": "some some discussions kind of suggests that as models get better, like the harness disappears. But I'm starting to think that's not true. I think we need both",
      "timestamp_sec": 928,
      "why": "Direct disagreement with a widely held position about harnesses."
